---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import heroBand from "../assets/hero-band.jpg";
---

<Layout title="Home" description="WaveForge: a collaborative music-education platform for distance learning.">
  <section class="hero">
    <Image
      src={heroBand}
      alt="Hands raising an acoustic guitar, a laptop, an electric guitar, a cymbal, a bass and a keyboard against a flat grey sky"
      class="hero-image"
      widths={[480, 960, 1600, 2400]}
      sizes="100vw"
      loading="eager"
    />
    <div class="hero-overlay">
      <div class="hero-section">
        <h1>Distance shouldn't mean nobody sings together.</h1>
        <p>
          Real internet latency makes playing in perfect sync impossible over distance — no app changes that.
          WaveForge doesn't pretend otherwise: it's built for call-and-response, the way a singing lesson or a round
          already works, so a teacher or a lead voice can model a phrase and everyone else can answer, from wherever
          they are.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="btn" href="/signup/">Create an account</a>
          <a class="btn secondary" href="/login/">Log in</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-section">
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <p>
          One person leads — a phrase, a line, a verse — and the room answers in turn, or layers a part on top once
          they've heard it. Everything else follows from that one decision.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Call-and-response, not synced playback: one leader at a time, never a race to be first.</li>
          <li>Camera and mic only turn on when you say so — a consent step every time you join a room, not just the first.</li>
          <li>"Original Sound" is off by default and raw when you ask for it: no echo cancellation or noise suppression fighting two people singing together.</li>
          <li>The server holds the one true copy of what the room looks like — a composition edit or a lyrics cue reaches everyone the same way, not whoever's connection happened to be fastest.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-section">
        <h2>What's here</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Accounts, and rooms you create or join by link.</li>
          <li>Camera and mic by explicit consent, with Original Sound for singing over each other.</li>
          <li>Lyrics you upload, with a karaoke-style cursor that follows whoever's leading.</li>
          <li>A shared composition space — real staff notation, not a grid of buttons — with one-click PDF export of what you wrote.</li>
          <li>Recordings of your own session, and of the room's group sound, saved straight to your device.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="hero-credit">Photo: Caleb George / Unsplash</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stats-strip" aria-label="WaveForge activity so far">
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-number" id="stat-rooms">—</span>
      <span class="stat-label">rooms created</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-number" id="stat-notes">—</span>
      <span class="stat-label">notes composed</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-number" id="stat-lyrics">—</span>
      <span class="stat-label">lyric sheets shared</span>
    </div>
  </section>
  <script>
    import { initLandingStats } from "../lib/landing-stats";
    initLandingStats();
  </script>
</Layout>
